# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

This resource manages a project-level check definition. It does not execute tests, create a GitHub workflow or webhook endpoint, or report check-run results. Configure the corresponding GitHub workflow, external runner, or integration separately.

## Example Usage

### GitHub and webhook checks

Use a Vercel access token authorized for the project. GitHub checks currently support only `github`; `external_check_name` must match the check reported by the workflow. GitHub checks cannot be rerequestable through Vercel.

{{ tffile "examples/resources/vercel_project_deployment_check/resource.tf" }}

### Integration checks

Integration checks require an integration installation token. The API identifies the integration and installation from that token. Use this token only with an `integration` source because it determines the returned source kind. The optional `external_resource_id` must belong to that installation.

{{ tffile "examples/resources/vercel_project_deployment_check/integration.tf" }}

## Choosing check behavior

- `requires` selects when the check can run: `build-ready`, `deployment-url`, or `none`.
- `blocks = "deployment-alias"` gates domain assignment; `blocks = "none"` makes the check non-blocking. Omission uses the API default, `deployment-alias`.
- `targets` accepts `production`, `preview`, or custom environment IDs (not slugs). For a custom environment managed by Terraform, use `targets = [vercel_custom_environment.staging.id]`. Omission defaults to production. Use `["all"]` to select every environment; do not combine `all` with other targets or supply an empty set.
- `is_rerequestable` defaults to false. Enable it only when your webhook runner or integration handles rerun requests.
- Optional computed settings are populated from the API. Omitting a previously configured setting keeps its current value; it does not reset the setting to its creation default.

Changing `project_id`, an explicitly configured `team_id`, or a configured source field replaces the check. Changing `requires` to `none` also replaces it because the API does not support that PATCH operation. Other supported settings update in place. Replacement deletes the old check and creates a new check with a new ID.

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Import

Use `team_id/project_id/check_id`, or `project_id/check_id` when the provider supplies the team. Project names are supported; the imported identifier must match the `project_id` value in configuration to avoid replacement.

{{ codefile "shell" .ImportFile }}

For Terraform 1.5 and later:

```terraform
import {
  to = vercel_project_deployment_check.existing
  id = "team_xxx/my-project/check_xxx"
}

resource "vercel_project_deployment_check" "existing" {
  project_id = "my-project"
  name       = "Existing deployment check"
  requires   = "deployment-url"
}
```

Match `name` and `requires` to the existing check before applying. Omit `source` to preserve an imported source, including Vercel-managed checks and Git-provider variants that this resource cannot create. Likewise, omit `blocks` to preserve an API value outside the configurable `deployment-alias` and `none` values. Inspect the imported state with `terraform state show` before configuring additional fields.

A source is required for new checks. For an imported check with an omitted source, configuring a different source replaces it. Supply a writable source before changing `requires` to `none`; response-only sources cannot be recreated by this resource.
